{"name":"Loch Modan Moss","hex":"#dfe5bf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"loch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lake.","A bay or arm of the sea."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of lohoch (“medicine taken by licking”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/loch"},{"term":"moss","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various small, green, seedless plants growing on the ground or on the surfaces of trees, stones, etc.; now specifically, a plant of the phylum Bryophyta (formerly division Musci).","A kind or species of such plants."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with moss.","To cover (something) with moss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moss"}],"entries":[{"title":"Loch","blocks":[{"text":"Loch is a word meaning \"lake\" or \"sea inlet\" in Scottish and Irish Gaelic, subsequently borrowed into English. In Irish contexts, it often appears in the anglicized form \"lough\". A small loch is sometimes called a lochan. Lochs which connect to the sea may be called \"sea lochs\" or \"sea loughs\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Ben_Lomond_Scotland.jpg/330px-Ben_Lomond_Scotland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loch","wikidata":"Q1172903"},{"title":"Saint Modan","blocks":[{"text":"St Modan was the son of an Irish chieftain. He became a monk and built a chapel at Dryburgh, Scotland, in 522 which he used as a base for several years. This later became the site of a monastery: Dryburgh Abbey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/GlasgowButeHallStModan.jpg/330px-GlasgowButeHallStModan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Modan","wikidata":"Q4299297"},{"title":"Moss","blocks":[{"text":"Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, bryophytes, which comprises liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2–10 cm (0.1–3.9 in) tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm (24 in) in height. There are approximately 12,000 species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg/330px-Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss","wikidata":"Q25347"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/LochLong%28StevePartridge%29Nov2007.jpg/330px-LochLong%28StevePartridge%29Nov2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/LochLong%28StevePartridge%29Nov2007.jpg/330px-LochLong%28StevePartridge%29Nov2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Looking down Loch Long, a long sea loch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALochLong(StevePartridge)Nov2007.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Loch_Lubnaig_%28223068248%29.jpg/500px-Loch_Lubnaig_%28223068248%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Loch_Lubnaig_%28223068248%29.jpg/1280px-Loch_Lubnaig_%28223068248%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Loch_Lubnaig_%28223068248%29.jpg/500px-Loch_Lubnaig_%28223068248%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Loch Lubnaig, a reservoir","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALoch_Lubnaig_(223068248).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lake_of_Menteith_looking_towards_Port_of_Monteith.jpg/500px-Lake_of_Menteith_looking_towards_Port_of_Monteith.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Lake_of_Menteith_looking_towards_Port_of_Monteith.jpg/500px-Lake_of_Menteith_looking_towards_Port_of_Monteith.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Lake of Menteith (Loch Innis MoCholmaig)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALake_of_Menteith_looking_towards_Port_of_Monteith.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Loch_Derculich_near_Aberfeldy%2C_Perthshire%2C_Scotland.JPG/500px-Loch_Derculich_near_Aberfeldy%2C_Perthshire%2C_Scotland.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Loch_Derculich_near_Aberfeldy%2C_Perthshire%2C_Scotland.JPG/1280px-Loch_Derculich_near_Aberfeldy%2C_Perthshire%2C_Scotland.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Loch_Derculich_near_Aberfeldy%2C_Perthshire%2C_Scotland.JPG/500px-Loch_Derculich_near_Aberfeldy%2C_Perthshire%2C_Scotland.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","a